# Test: `show firewall ruleset <table>` over the real SSH `ze cli` path names the
#       DNS name that supplied each address in a domain group's set.
# Spec: plan/spec-firewall-domain-group.md -- Wiring Test row 5, user story 3
# VALIDATES: the enrichment reaches an operator. handleShowFirewallRuleset
#            (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/cmd_show.go) calls show.Enrich, the
#            hub forwards `enrich-show` to the domain plugin, enrichShow
#            (internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/enrich.go) attaches
#            source-name to every element it resolved, and the merged answer is
#            rendered to the client.
# PREVENTS: the enrichment being proven at the enricher alone. enrichShow
#           returning the right map says nothing about whether the registration
#           key matches the command string the show handler passes, whether the
#           merge survives the plugin boundary, or whether the name reaches the
#           client at all. This file is the only assertion made from outside the
#           daemon.
#
# NEVER EXECUTED. Written on 2026-09-06 and not run once. The command that runs
# it is `./le functional firewall`; on a host without CAP_NET_ADMIN it is
# skipped by the option below, and the runner that does execute it is
# `./le qemu netns-test suites firewall`.
#
# THE GO FIXTURE IS NOT WRITTEN EITHER. `ze-test fixture
# firewall/firewall-cli-domain-group-show` does not exist yet: it is named under
# "Files to Create" in the spec and it is still owed. Until it exists this file
# fails at command 2 for that reason and for no other. What the fixture MUST do,
# in this order, is what the assertions below are written against:
#   1. Serve DNS on 127.0.0.1:53, answering web.example.test with A
#      203.0.113.10.
#   2. Provision client credentials with `ze init` into a sandbox config dir,
#      the way test/firewall/firewall-cli-show.ci's fixture does.
#   3. Dispatch `update firewall domain-group web`, so the group has data.
#   4. Commit, over `ze cli`, a term in table fw_dg matching
#      source-domain-group web. It is committed HERE and not in the config
#      below because a commit naming a never-resolved group is refused
#      (OnConfigVerify), which is the order user story 1 already describes.
#   5. Run `show firewall ruleset fw_dg` over `ze cli` and print the answer.
#
# ONE SCENARIO PER FILE. An expect=stdout: assertion is FILE-level: the runner
# checks it against the concatenated output of every command in the file
# (runner_exec.go, rec.ClientOutput), stdout and stderr together. Nothing here
# asserts an absence, so the whole file reads one command's answer.
#
# Auth setup mirrors test/firewall/firewall-cli-show.ci: the daemon config
# declares a super-admin user and an SSH server, and the fixture drives `ze cli`
# against it. The SSH port is fixed because the per-test netns isolates it.
#
# This test programs nftables, so it needs CAP_NET_ADMIN, not merely Linux.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# Serialize against every other firewall-domain-group test: the nftables ruleset
# is NODE-WIDE and the set names come from the group name (setNames, sets.go).
option=exclusive:group=firewall-domain-group-nft
option=timeout:value=30s

stdin=ze-fw:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	# The hub resolver reads NameServers[0] as its server (newResolvers,
	# cmd/ze/hub/main_system.go), so this points every lookup at the fixture's
	# stub rather than at the host's real DNS.
	name-server 127.0.0.1;
	dns {
		resolv-conf-path "";
	}
	authentication {
		user operator {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO";
		}
	}
}

firewall {
	backend nft;
	domain-group web {
		domain-names [ web.example.test ];
		ttl-floor 60;
	}
	table fw_dg {
		family inet;
		chain input {
			type filter;
			hook input;
			priority 0;
			policy accept;
			# A drop-policy chain would lock out the `ze cli` -> SSH connection
			# this test is driven over, so the policy is accept and the term the
			# enrichment is read from is committed by the fixture.
			term allow-loopback {
				from { input-interface lo; }
				then { accept; }
			}
		}
	}
}

environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true;
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port 2222;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-fw
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture firewall/firewall-cli-domain-group-show:timeout=25s
expect=exit:code=0

# The set and its address are in the answer at all. Read back from the applied
# kernel state by the show handler, so an in-memory copy fails here.
expect=stdout:contains=domain_v4_web
expect=stdout:contains=203.0.113.10

# The name is attached to THAT address, and not merely somewhere in the same
# document. `[^}]*` keeps both inside one element object, so a product that
# rendered the group's names in a separate list, or attached the name to the
# wrong element, fails this line while passing the two above. The alternation is
# there because the key order of a rendered map is not this test's business:
# source-name is the field enrichSet adds beside value (enrich.go), and either
# order says the same thing.
expect=stdout:pattern=(203\.0\.113\.10[^}]*web\.example\.test|web\.example\.test[^}]*203\.0\.113\.10)
